.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt NOVI-VERIFY 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm novi-verify
.Nd check an Ed25519 signature
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar public-key
.Ar signature
.Ar file
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
verifies that
.Ar signature
is a valid Ed25519 signature over
.Ar file
by the key in
.Ar public-key .
It prints nothing on success.
.Pp
It is the trust root of the package system:
.Xr pkg 1
runs it over the repository index before believing a line of it, and
every package's SHA-256 is inside that signed index.
Everything the machine installs depends on this program being right.
.Ss Why it is static, and why TweetNaCl is pinned
Checking a signature must not need a TLS stack.
.Nm
is statically linked against a hash-pinned TweetNaCl \(em the only
pinned source in this project.
Every other dependency is trusted for where it comes from; this one
.Em is
the trust, so a modified TweetNaCl would mean signatures verified by
an implementation an attacker chose.
.Pp
A missing signature is not weaker than a wrong one.
Both fail, and the decision lives in one function, because two places
that decide it is how one of them ends up more permissive.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/etc/novi/keys/novi.pub" -compact
.It Pa /etc/novi/keys/novi.pub
The repository signing key.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Exit 0 means the signature is good.
Any other status means it is not, whatever the reason: a bad
signature, a missing file, a key of the wrong length.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ novi-verify /etc/novi/keys/novi.pub index.sig index && echo good
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pkg 1
.Sh STANDARDS
RFC 0006.
